WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.070
Welcome candidates.

2
00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:03.267
So, you're in the final stretch, right?

3
00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:07.999
I know exactly how immense the pressure is right now during this final review phase.

4
00:00:07.999 --> 00:00:10.928
And well, I know exactly what's on the line for you.

5
00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:13.913
Today, we aren't just going to rehash basic concepts.

6
00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:14.308
No way.

7
00:00:14.308 --> 00:00:23.940
We're going to equip you with the exact step-by-step frameworks you need to navigate the absolute trickiest disability income and long-term care questions on the CFP exam.

8
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:31.094
All right, let's dive right into this explainer and get you those critical points because here's the harsh reality of test day.

9
00:00:31.094 --> 00:00:35.037
Just knowing the basic definitions, it's honestly not going to cut it.

10
00:00:35.037 --> 00:00:40.782
To actually pass, you have to be able to spot the very specific traps they've designed to trip you up.

11
00:00:40.782 --> 00:00:47.711
The board absolutely loves to test the exceptions, the inverse rules, and the exact precise phrasing of insurance triggers.

12
00:00:47.711 --> 00:00:54.865
So, we're going to aggressively conquer these areas today so you can walk into that exam room with total unwavering confidence.

13
00:00:54.865 --> 00:00:55.146
Okay.

14
00:00:55.146 --> 00:00:57.230
So, here's our battle plan for today.

15
00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:05.060
We're going to cover one, ranking definitions, two, periods, three, the taxation trap, four, LTC benefit triggers, and finally number five,

16
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.525
high yield exam takeaways.

17
00:01:06.525 --> 00:01:11.932
All right, let's jump straight into section one, ranking definitions from broadest to narrowest.

18
00:01:11.932 --> 00:01:17.396
You see, the definition of disability in a policy is literally the single most important feature,

19
00:01:17.396 --> 00:01:22.973
mostly because it heavily dictates premium costs and ultimately when the benefits actually pay out.

20
00:01:22.973 --> 00:01:30.127
You absolutely must memorize this strict hierarchy, stepping down from the most generous all the way to the absolute strictest.

21
00:01:30.127 --> 00:01:32.718
So, at the very top, we've got own occupation.

22
00:01:32.718 --> 00:01:36.717
This is the broadest and as you'd guess, the most expensive definition.

23
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:41.899
Basically, if you can't perform the principal duties of your own specific job, you get paid.

24
00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:46.744
Right below that is modified own occupation, which is then followed by any occupation.

25
00:01:46.744 --> 00:01:53.672
And finally, sitting at the very bottom, the absolute narrowest, strictest definition on the entire board, social security.

26
00:01:53.672 --> 00:02:02.009
This one actually requires an inability to engage in any substantial gainful activity that's expected to last at least 12 months or result in death.

27
00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:07.811
Now, let's look really closely at a classic exam trap, which contrasts modified Own Occ versus Any Occ.

28
00:02:07.811 --> 00:02:08.543
Picture this.

29
00:02:08.543 --> 00:02:12.880
Let's say you have a highly paid trial attorney who suffers an injury, right?

30
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.161
And they can no longer handle the rigorous day-to-day demands of trial work.

31
00:02:17.161 --> 00:02:18.457
Under modified Own Occ,

32
00:02:18.457 --> 00:02:25.385
they're considered disabled because they can't do the job they are specifically fitted for by their education and training.

33
00:02:25.385 --> 00:02:31.920
But under Any Occ, man, if that exact same attorney is physically able to say take orders at a fast food restaurant,

34
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.130
they are completely disqualified from receiving benefits.

35
00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.566
And that's despite the massive, massive drop in their income.

36
00:02:38.566 --> 00:02:43.692
So, you really have to read the question carefully to see exactly which definition applies.

37
00:02:43.692 --> 00:02:48.424
Moving right along to section two, periods, specifically elimination versus benefit.

38
00:02:48.424 --> 00:02:56.197
Now, this is a highly tested area where candidates frequently and honestly understandably confuse the waiting phase with the payout phase.

39
00:02:56.197 --> 00:02:57.155
Look at day zero.

40
00:02:57.155 --> 00:02:59.972
That's when the injury or illness actually occurs.

41
00:02:59.972 --> 00:03:01.042
But listen closely.

42
00:03:01.042 --> 00:03:03.070
The policy does not pay immediately.

43
00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:07.013
First, the client has to survive what's called the elimination period.

44
00:03:07.013 --> 00:03:09.266
Think of this as purely a waiting phase.

45
00:03:09.266 --> 00:03:17.434
It's only after that elimination period is completely 100% satisfied that the benefit period begins and the actual cash payouts finally commence.

46
00:03:17.434 --> 00:03:22.503
So, the absolute crucial point you need to remember is this exact phrase, time deductible.

47
00:03:22.503 --> 00:03:25.151
Seriously, burn that into your brain right now.

48
00:03:25.151 --> 00:03:27.629
The elimination period is a time deductible.

49
00:03:27.629 --> 00:03:32.924
Just like you'd pay a literal dollar amount out of pocket before your auto insurance kicks in,

50
00:03:32.924 --> 00:03:36.754
your client pays in time before their disability insurance kicks in.

51
00:03:36.754 --> 00:03:40.191
In individual policies, this is typically 30, 60, or 90 days.

52
00:03:40.191 --> 00:03:44.753
So, to put that into perspective, if your client has a 90-day elimination period,

53
00:03:44.753 --> 00:03:49.541
they will literally not see a single dime for the first 3 months of their disability.

54
00:03:49.541 --> 00:03:52.358
All right, on to section three, the taxation trap.

55
00:03:52.358 --> 00:03:56.977
If there is just one single thing you take away from today, please let it be this.

56
00:03:56.977 --> 00:04:03.792
The exam absolutely loves testing the inverse relationship between who pays the premium and how those benefits are taxed.

57
00:04:03.792 --> 00:04:06.553
You simply cannot have it both ways with the IRS.

58
00:04:06.553 --> 00:04:07.341
It's a seesaw.

59
00:04:07.341 --> 00:04:11.510
If the premium is paid by the employer and deducted as a business expense,

60
00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:15.565
then the resulting benefit to the employee is going to be fully taxable.

61
00:04:15.565 --> 00:04:22.325
Conversely, if the employee pays the premium straight out of their own pocket with after tax dollars, meaning, you know,

62
00:04:22.325 --> 00:04:28.465
they take no deduction up front, then the ultimate disability benefit they receive is entirely 100% tax-free.

63
00:04:28.465 --> 00:04:29.141
It's simple.

64
00:04:29.141 --> 00:04:31.563
Deduct the premium, pay tax on the benefit.

65
00:04:31.563 --> 00:04:34.323
Don't deduct the premium, get a tax-free benefit.

66
00:04:34.323 --> 00:04:34.830
But wait.

67
00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:39.843
Because here comes the classic curveball trap that catches so many test takers off-guard.

68
00:04:39.843 --> 00:04:42.941
I'm talking about the S-corp and partnership exception.

69
00:04:42.941 --> 00:04:47.673
For partners or greater than 2% S-corp owners, the rules have a sneaky little twist.

70
00:04:47.673 --> 00:04:50.151
The business entity does deduct the premium.

71
00:04:50.151 --> 00:04:55.953
Sure, but it must attribute that exact premium amount as taxable income to the owner on their W2 or K1.

72
00:04:55.953 --> 00:05:03.332
And because the owner was taxed on the premium upfront, the eventual disability benefit they receive magically becomes non-taxable.

73
00:05:03.332 --> 00:05:09.472
You really have to read carefully to see if your client is just a standard employee or if they're a 2% owner.

74
00:05:09.472 --> 00:05:12.627
Okay, let's pivot to section four, LTC benefit triggers.

75
00:05:12.627 --> 00:05:17.246
So, we are shifting gears here from replacing income to paying for long-term care.

76
00:05:17.246 --> 00:05:20.006
I want you to remember this specific number, two,

77
00:05:20.006 --> 00:05:29.807
the very first trigger for a HIPAA qualified tax advantaged LTC policy requires the insured to need substantial assistance with two out of the six activities of daily living,

78
00:05:29.807 --> 00:05:31.497
or ADLs, for at least 90 days.

79
00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:34.820
And what exactly are those six specific HIPAA defined ADLs?

80
00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:39.101
They are bathing, continence, dressing, eating, toileting, and transferring.

81
00:05:39.101 --> 00:05:42.256
You know, like physically getting from a bed to a chair.

82
00:05:42.256 --> 00:05:46.199
But take a close look at what is conspicuously missing from this list.

83
00:05:46.199 --> 00:05:48.508
Medical necessity is nowhere to be found.

84
00:05:48.508 --> 00:05:55.211
The exam will definitely try to trick you by throwing in a scenario where a doctor deemed the care a medical necessity.

85
00:05:55.211 --> 00:05:58.591
Under HIPAA for tax qualified policies, that is a huge trap.

86
00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:00.450
It is simply not a valid trigger.

87
00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:03.435
It absolutely must be two of these six specific ADLs.

88
00:06:03.435 --> 00:06:10.364
But hold on, imagine a scenario where a client is perfectly capable of physically bathing, eating, and dressing themselves,

89
00:06:10.364 --> 00:06:13.800
but they are sadly suffering from severe Alzheimer's disease.

90
00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:19.827
They don't meet that physical ADL requirement we just talked about, but they clearly desperately need care.

91
00:06:19.827 --> 00:06:20.841
What happens then?

92
00:06:20.841 --> 00:06:26.192
Well, this is exactly where the second major LTC trigger comes into play, cognitive impairment.

93
00:06:26.192 --> 00:06:29.122
If an individual has severe dementia or Alzheimer's,

94
00:06:29.122 --> 00:06:36.050
they can qualify for benefits if they require substantial services to protect them from threats to their health and safety.

95
00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:42.528
The absolute key phrasing you want to scan for here is protecting the individual from threats to health and safety.

96
00:06:42.528 --> 00:06:46.302
That is an alternate equally testable trigger to the physical ADLs.

97
00:06:46.302 --> 00:06:47.541
So do not overlook it.

98
00:06:47.541 --> 00:06:51.428
Let's bring it all home with section five, high yield exam takeaways.

99
00:06:51.428 --> 00:06:53.512
All right, this is your final sprint.

100
00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:54.188
Let's recap.

101
00:06:54.188 --> 00:06:56.667
One, you got to know the definition ranking.

102
00:06:56.667 --> 00:06:58.018
Own Occ is the broadest.

103
00:06:58.018 --> 00:07:00.384
Social Security is the absolute narrowest.

104
00:07:00.384 --> 00:07:01.961
Two, the elimination period.

105
00:07:01.961 --> 00:07:04.215
Just remember it's your time deductible.

106
00:07:04.215 --> 00:07:07.989
Three, if the employer pays the DI premium, the benefit is taxable.

107
00:07:07.989 --> 00:07:12.213
Four, if the employee pays with after tax dollars, the benefit is tax-free.

108
00:07:12.213 --> 00:07:19.649
And finally, five, your HIPAA qualified LTC triggers are an inability to perform two of the six ADLs or severe cognitive impairment.

109
00:07:19.649 --> 00:07:24.718
You know, at the end of the day, earning your CFP marks isn't just some academic exercise.

110
00:07:24.718 --> 00:07:31.591
Yes, you are studying your heart out to pass a rigorous exam, but ultimately, you're doing this to master risk management.

111
00:07:31.591 --> 00:07:36.773
So you can protect real world clients from catastrophic financial ruin, a sudden disability,

112
00:07:36.773 --> 00:07:43.589
or an unexpected need for long-term care that can literally destroy decades of careful wealth accumulation in an instant.

113
00:07:43.589 --> 00:07:48.320
So, I'm going to leave you with this final thought as you head back into your books.

114
00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:55.925
When your future client is sitting right across from you, facing a life-altering disability or a truly devastating cognitive diagnosis,

115
00:07:55.925 --> 00:08:01.107
will you have built the risk-management blueprint that actually saves their family's future?

116
00:08:01.107 --> 00:08:02.515
Keep that weight in mind.

117
00:08:02.515 --> 00:08:05.163
Stay incredibly focused and go crush this exam.

118
00:08:05.163 --> 00:08:06.064
You've got this.

